alex: Quick heads-up — seeing a bunch of TIME_WAIT sockets in prod when we use an HttpClient-per-request pattern + Polly retry for streaming downloads. Anyone seen weird streaming/disposal interactions?

morgan: How are you creating the client and the retry policy? snippet helps.

alex: here's a simplified version:```csharp
public async Task DownloadAsync(string url, string path, CancellationToken ct) {
  using var client = new HttpClient();
  var policy = Policy.Handle<HttpRequestException>().WaitAndRetryAsync(3, _ => TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
  var response = await policy.ExecuteAsync(() => client.GetAsync(url, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead, ct));
  response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
  using var stream = await response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
  using var fs = File.Create(path);
  await stream.CopyToAsync(fs, 81920, ct);
}
```

ria: couple of immediate flags: 1) creating/disposing HttpClient per request -> socket churn. Use IHttpClientFactory or a singleton. 2) Polly wrapping the GetAsync that returns a response with a live stream can be tricky: if retry happens you need to recreate HttpRequestMessage for each try and cannot reuse the same response/stream.

jamal: Agree. Also when you use ResponseHeadersRead you get the stream that must be consumed by the caller; if a transient failure happens while copying the stream you'll need logic to abort and re-download (server might have partial content). Retries are safest at the request-send level for idempotent ops, but be careful to recreate HttpRequestMessage each attempt.

morgan: Another practical point: HttpClient.Timeout defaults to 100s. For long streaming downloads it's common to set client.Timeout = Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan and instead rely on cancellation tokens and per-request timeouts. Also check SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime/PooledConnectionIdleTimeout to recycle stale sockets.

alex: Good tips. For Polly, is it ok to use a Policy<HttpResponseMessage> as a handler via AddPolicyHandler? I was thinking AddPolicyHandler(Policy<HttpResponseMessage>.HandleTransientHttpError().RetryAsync(2)).

ria: That works if you let the client factory create the request each time and the policy is applied as an outgoing handler. Example DI registration:```csharp
services.AddHttpClient("downloader")
    .ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler(() => new SocketsHttpHandler { PooledConnectionLifetime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5) })
    .AddPolicyHandler(Policy<HttpResponseMessage>.HandleTransientHttpError().RetryAsync(2));
```
Then use IHttpClientFactory.CreateClient("downloader") and call SendAsync with a fresh HttpRequestMessage each time:```csharp
var req = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url);
var resp = await client.SendAsync(req, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead, ct);
resp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
using var stream = await resp.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
// copy...
```

jamal: One more nuance: If you use a DelegatingHandler or the AddPolicyHandler from Polly.Extensions.Http, the response object is returned after policy success. That is fine, but make sure your retry policy only handles appropriate transient conditions (5xx, 408, network faults). If the policy retries on a thrown exception after the response stream is already partially read you may leak resources. So: 1) limit retries to before the stream consumption step, 2) or perform read-within-a-second-layer retry that restarts the whole request (and recreates file target or resumes using range requests).

morgan: For diagnostics: on the host check `ss -tanp | grep dotnet` (or netstat) to see socket states, and use `dotnet counters` / EventPipe to monitor connection pool stats. Also a quick code check: do you ever call response.Dispose() after starting async CopyTo? disposing too early can cancel the underlying socket and leak TIME_WAITs.

alex: We're currently disposing after CopyTo completes. But we are creating clients per call. Will switch to named client + IHttpClientFactory and move retry to handler level. Also will set Timeout to infinite and tune pooled connection lifetime.

ria: Sounds good. If you want to avoid any mid-stream retry complexity, treat streaming as a single-shot operation: allow network-level retries for SYN/connect, but once the body is streaming fail and restart the download (maybe write to a temp file and replace on success). For very large files consider Range headers and resumable downloads.

alex: Great — I'll implement the named client + AddPolicyHandler approach and open a PR with the updated downloader and some unit/integration tests for retry behavior. Will also add a small e2e test that simulates a server drop mid-stream.

morgan: Ping me on the PR for a quick review. Also add a small note in README about using ResponseHeadersRead + Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan so consumers don't copy the anti-pattern.

alex: Will do. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:

